The new <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/03/04/apple-announces-low-cost-macbook-neo-with-a18-pro-chip/">MacBook Neo</a> is equipped with two USB-C ports, but they are <a href="https://www.apple.com/macbook-neo/specs/">not the same</a>. The left USB-C port supports USB 3 speeds of up to 10 Gb/s, while the right USB-C port closer to the trackpad is limited to USB 2 speeds of just 480 Mb/s. As a result, Apple says external display connectivity is supported on the left port only.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/03/macbook-neo-ports.jpg" alt="" width="2000" height="1125" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1038579" /><br/>
Given the ports are not labeled, this limitation could be an inconvenience. Fortunately, though, <a href="https://daringfireball.net/2026/03/599_not_a_piece_of_junk_macbook_neo"><em>Daring Fireball</em>'s John Gruber said</a> if you plug an external display into the incorrect port, macOS will alert you to use the other port.<br/>
<br/>
Apple says the MacBook Neo <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/03/04/macbook-neo-one-external-display/">supports one external display</a> with up to 4K resolution at 60Hz.<br/>
